# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env, and never put real
# credentials in this example file.
DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./scraper.db
# MySQL example (note the placeholder — substitute your own):
# DATABASE_URL=mysql+pymysql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:3306/DBNAME
ADMIN_API_KEY=replace-me-with-a-long-random-string
APPROVED_PARTICIPANTS_CSV=./approved_participants.csv
# Pairing a shared link to a passively captured post by TIME ALONE,
# within this many seconds. 0 disables it, and 0 is the answer.
#
# It shipped here as 900 and every .env copied from this file has it
# on, which is how a platform whose links pair exactly ended up with
# `linked (by time)` on every row -- and with one video's 抖音号 or
# @handle beside another video's caption and counts. One TikTok table:
# the handle `rulebreaker2424` against the display names Xena, Jenn,
# MamaCann and aulliv, and the handle `jedd64117` against Rule
# Breaker🌈. The link and the screen reading were different videos.
#
# The cost is not a cosmetic one. The id a row carries is what the
# takedown check re-visits, so a wrongly paired row reports the
# survival of a video other than the one whose caption is beside it.
# Better to leave the row without an id and say so.
#
# Exact pairing -- the device naming the post the share sheet was
# opened on -- needs no window and is unaffected by this.
# `python -m app.pairing --undo-time-pairings` takes back ids already
# attached this way. See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and docs/METHODOLOGY.md §3.
PAIRING_WINDOW_SECONDS=0
